/* ==============================================================
   01 — WRF: the World Reference Frame
   --------------------------------------------------------------
   ROBOT     Meca500 only (R3 or R4)
   TEACHES   SetWrf — moving the origin instead of the program
   NEEDS     Robot activated + homed, no tool mounted
   WATCH     The same four lines of code, run in two places
   TIME      ~30 s
   ============================================================== */

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   THE PROBLEM IT SOLVES

   Every Cartesian pose you write is measured from somewhere. By
   default that somewhere is the BRF — the Base Reference Frame,
   fixed in the robot's own base.

   Which means every coordinate in your program is secretly a
   statement about where the robot is bolted down. Move the robot
   20 mm on the bench, or build a second identical cell whose
   fixture sits a little differently, and every pose in the file
   is now wrong.

       SetWrf(x, y, z, alpha, beta, gamma)

   defines the WRF — the World Reference Frame — with respect to
   the BRF. From then on, every MovePose and MoveLin is measured
   from the WRF instead.

   Put the WRF on the fixture, the pallet, the tray, the machine
   you are tending. Now your coordinates describe the WORK, not
   the robot. Write the poses once, relative to the fixture, and
   commission a new cell by measuring one frame.

   THE PAYOFF, IN THE WORDS A REAL APPLICATION USES

     "We moved the robot and nothing had to be reprogrammed."
     "We have six identical stations and one program."
     "The fixture is on a shuttle and we index the frame."

   All three are the same feature.

   BY DEFAULT

   The WRF coincides with the BRF, so if you never call SetWrf,
   everything is measured from the robot base and nothing is
   surprising. Note that the WRF resets to that default every
   time the robot is activated or reactivated — a program that
   depends on a frame must set the frame.

   THE MENTAL MODEL

   SetWrf does not move the robot. It changes what the numbers
   MEAN. The line MovePose(50, 0, 200, 0, 90, 0) sends the tool
   to a different physical place before and after a SetWrf, and
   that is exactly the point.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */


/* --- Standard reset: start from a known state -----------------
   Deactivating loses every NON-persistent setting — tool and
   world frames, speeds, accelerations, blending, payload, torque
   limits, posture and turn selection, move mode — so this
   program starts from firmware defaults no matter what ran
   before it. Reactivating brings the motors back.

   This does NOT move the arm: a robot that was already homed
   needs no re-homing. (With an MEGP 25* gripper fitted, the
   gripper re-homes on reactivation. The robot still does not
   move — but keep the fingers clear.)

   It does NOT reset persistent settings — joint limits, work
   zone, tool sphere, collision and calibration config all
   survive. Run common/cold_start.mxprog for those.

   This block is identical in every example. To remove it
   everywhere, see the README. */
DeactivateRobot()
ActivateRobot()
Home()
ResetError()
ResumeMotion()


SetJointVel(20)
SetCartLinVel(80)

MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0)
Delay(1)


/* --- Station A -------------------------------------------------
   Put the world origin 150 mm out in X and 200 mm up, roughly
   in front of the robot. Imagine a fixture bolted there.

   In a real cell these six numbers come from measurement, or
   from jogging the robot to the fixture's corner and reading the
   pose off the MecaPortal. */
SetWrf(150, -60, 200, 0, 0, 0)

// Everything below is written relative to that fixture. Note
// that these are small, human-sized numbers: "40 mm above the
// corner", "60 mm along the edge". Nobody has to think about
// where the robot base is.
MovePose(0, 0, 80, 0, 90, 0)      // above the fixture corner
MoveLin(0, 0, 40, 0, 90, 0)       // descend to the part
Delay(0.5)
MoveLin(60, 0, 40, 0, 90, 0)      // traverse along the edge
MoveLin(60, 0, 80, 0, 90, 0)      // lift clear

Delay(1)


/* --- Station B -------------------------------------------------
   The second fixture is 120 mm across from the first and rotated
   by 30 degrees. One line describes that. */
SetWrf(150, 60, 200, 0, 0, 30)

// Now the interesting part: the five motion lines below are
// character-for-character identical to the five above. Copy and
// paste, nothing edited. They execute at the second fixture, in
// its own rotated orientation, because the frame moved.
//
// This is the demo. Do not explain it first — run it, let them
// see the robot work two different stations, and then scroll up
// and show that the code is the same.
MovePose(0, 0, 80, 0, 90, 0)
MoveLin(0, 0, 40, 0, 90, 0)
Delay(0.5)
MoveLin(60, 0, 40, 0, 90, 0)
MoveLin(60, 0, 80, 0, 90, 0)


// --- Put the world back ---------------------------------------
// Reset the WRF to the base frame, so the next example starts
// from a known state. Forgetting this is a classic way to
// confuse yourself in a shared demo cell.
SetWrf(0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0)

MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0)


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   THINGS TO TRY

   1. Change one number in the second SetWrf — say the rotation,
      from 30 to 60 — and run again. The whole station rotates.
      Ask how long that change would take on their current
      system.

   2. Turn on the WRF display in the 3D view panel. Seeing the
      frame jump between stations makes the abstraction concrete
      for people who do not read code.

   NEXT   02_tool_reference_frame.mxprog — the other frame, and
          the one that changes when the gripper does.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */
